    38	## 5. Pre-registered hypotheses and decision rules
    39	
    40	Statistics as exp_03/exp_04: per query, the five per-seed errors are averaged (plain means; a query enters a cell only if finite for all seeds in both arms of the cell); paired per-query differences bootstrapped over queries (20 000 resamples, bootstrap seed 0; query-level primary, this fixed split) with the room-cluster bootstrap (17 rooms) secondary; two-seed convergence gate (max endpoint movement ≤ 10 % of the seed-0 width, else raise `n_boot`). Each hypothesis is a separate family; no omnibus rule.
    41	
    42	- **H1 (dominance of the curve; K = 8, EDT and C50 error, epoch 12):** for every tier t ∈ {S, M, L}, δ_t = (mean error_cyl,t − mean error_simple,t) / mean error_simple,t (recomputed inside each paired resample). Family m = 6 (3 tiers × 2 metrics), two-sided adjusted intervals [Q_{0.05/12}, Q_{1−0.05/12}], bootstrap seeds 0/1 with the convergence gate. Verdict per metric: "dominance supported on <metric> at the three tested tiers" iff the adjusted upper bound of δ_t is below 0 at all three tiers; "partial" if at some; "not supported" if none — stated separately for EDT and C50, never as an unqualified "dominance", and never extrapolated between the tested capacities. T60 descriptive. The same table at K = 1 as a separate secondary family, **reported in full alongside K = 8** (all cells, intervals, cohorts and verdicts), with the analysis stating explicitly where the K = 1 conclusions differ from the K = 8 conclusions (exp_03's K = 1 follow-up showed the reversal at M; the curve tells whether it is tier-dependent). H2 is likewise computed and reported at K = 1 as a secondary four-cell family with the same rules.
    43	- **H2 (fixed-target reading; experiment 3): one four-cell family** = {Cyl-S vs Base-M, Cyl-M vs Base-L} × {EDT, C50} at K = 8. The target of a cell is the baseline arm's five-seed mean of the metric **computed on that cell's registered paired cohort** (queries finite for all five seeds in both arms of the cell) — the same cohort on which ρ = (mean error_cyl − mean error_base)/mean error_base is recomputed inside each paired resample (bootstrap seeds 0 and 1). The "reaches the target" claim and any parameter-ratio statement are scoped to that cohort; the baseline's own-cohort curve mean is shown separately and labelled, and the producer reports both together with the cohort sizes (a producer test uses the reviewer's counterexample — baseline [1, 1, 3, 3], cylindrical [invalid, invalid, 2.4, 2.6] — to assert that the target is 3 on the paired cohort, not 2). "Reaches the target" iff the superiority interval [Q_0.00625, Q_0.99375] has its upper endpoint below 0, **or** TOST equivalence within ±3 % holds with each one-sided test at α = 0.0125, i.e. [Q_0.0125, Q_0.9875] strictly inside (−0.03, +0.03). The encoder-parameter ratio is reported for a pairing only if both its metric cells reach the target (2 777 984 vs 19 703 296 ≈ 7.1×; 19 750 912 vs 43 709 184 ≈ 2.2×). Every decision-driving interval is covered by the two-seed convergence gate. No other pairing is tested.
    44	- **Cohorts (registered):** a plotted curve point, its seed SD and every fixed target use the arm's own cohort — the queries finite for all five seeds of that arm and metric; a comparison cell uses the paired cohort — queries finite for all five seeds in both arms of the cell. Both cohorts' sizes (queries and rooms) and the excluded queries per arm/seed/metric are reported; an empty cohort refuses the cell; where a plotted mean and a cell mean differ because of the cohorts, both are shown and labelled.
    45	- **Descriptive:** the two curves (x = encoder parameters, log scale; y = error with seed SD and bootstrap intervals), the full-system parameter axis as a second panel, per-epoch test-loss trajectories, the yaw-robustness block per tier, throughput and memory per tier.
