   329	def pad_batch(batch, batch_size):
   330	    """Repeat a short batch's rows so it is computed at exactly ``batch_size`` samples.
   331	
   332	    cuBLAS and cuDNN pick their algorithm -- and therefore their reduction order -- from
   333	    the *shape*, so a query's prediction depends on how many samples share its batch:
   334	    measured on a trained checkpoint, the same query evaluated alone and inside a batch
   335	    of 16 differs by 4.8e-5 in the log-spectrogram.  The split is 6337 = 396 * 16 + 1, so
   336	    without padding the last query would be the only one computed at a different shape.
   337	    Repeating rows (wrap-around) keeps the shape canonical; the padded rows are dropped
   338	    before anything is recorded, and because every op here is row-independent the kept
   339	    rows are bit-identical to their values in a full batch.
